event: answer
data: {"type":"answer","answer":{"summary":"Supported by the data: Company-specific: Dollar General's operating margin compressed while its SIC 5331 peers' margins held over the latest four fiscal years in the data; Cost structure: Dollar General's squeeze came from operating costs rising as a share of revenue, not from merchandise gross margin over the latest four fiscal years in the data. Refuted: Sector-wide. Inconclusive: Macro. Company figures cite each fiscal period end date (edgar fiscal_year = calendar year in which most of the fiscal year falls). Produced by Causa's deterministic sample analyst (SAMPLE_MODE: rule-based verdicts over real SEC EDGAR / World Bank rows, no LLM).","claims":[{"text":"Dollar General (DG)'s operating margin went from 8.79% (FY ending 2023-02-03) to 5.16% (FY ending 2026-01-30), a change of -3.63 percentage points, while its gross margin went from 31.23% to 30.66% [ev-1].","confidence":"high","evidenceIds":["ev-1"],"verified":true,"criticNote":"Critic: Sample critic (deterministic): 6 number(s) re-checked against the cited rows."},{"text":"Company-specific: over the same fiscal years its SIC 5331 peers' operating margins did not compress like DG's: COST 3.43% to 3.77%; TGT 3.53% to 4.88%; WMT 3.34% to 4.18% [ev-1].","confidence":"high","evidenceIds":["ev-1"],"verified":true,"criticNote":"Critic: Sample critic (deterministic): 6 number(s) re-checked against the cited rows."},{"text":"Cost structure: Dollar General (DG)'s operating costs below gross profit rose from 22.44% to 25.50% of revenue (FY ending 2023-02-03 to FY ending 2026-01-30) while its gross margin moved from 31.23% to 30.66%, so most of the squeeze came below gross profit [ev-3].","confidence":"high","evidenceIds":["ev-3"],"verified":true,"criticNote":"Critic: Sample critic (deterministic): 5 number(s) re-checked against the cited rows."},{"text":"Not sector-wide: the aggregate operating margin of Dollar General (DG)'s SIC 5331 peers excluding DG went from 3.39% in fiscal_year 2022 to 4.15% in fiscal_year 2025, while DG's went from 8.79% to 5.16% [ev-2].","confidence":"medium","evidenceIds":["ev-2"],"verified":true,"criticNote":"Critic: Sample critic (deterministic): 4 number(s) re-checked against the cited rows."}],"models":["causa/sample-analyst"]}}
